<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neuburg an der Donau</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Neuburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neuburg an der Donau is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. From 1505 it was the capital of the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a>, created for the grandsons of Duke George of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a>. Otto Henry rebuilt its castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style, and the town remained a Palatine residence until the line moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau.jpg" alt="Neuburg an der Donau" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Mendli at German Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_Neuburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Neuburg an der Donau is the capital of the Neuburg-Schrobenhausen district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and is more than 1,200 years old. People have lived on the hill where the palace now stands since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. A Roman settlement on the high hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> formed part of the Limes, the frontier between the Empire and its Germanic enemies. The municipality has 16 divisions, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, Bittenbrunn, Joshofen and Zell. Munich Airport is 82 km away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Aiglolfings built a massive castle in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> on the site of the old Roman fortress. Neuburg was part of an episcopal see, and in the 10th century it passed to the counts of Scheyern and through them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. It was ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-region">Rhenish Palatinate</a> at the close of a war. For a time it was the capital of a small principality, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a>, ruled by a cadet branch of the family of the elector palatine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, with an area of about 2,600 square kilometres and about 100,000 inhabitants. During the Thirty Years' War Swedish troops conquered and occupied the town several times between 1632 and 1634 while crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><p>In 1742 the principality was united again with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-region">Rhenish Palatinate</a>, with which it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1777. In 1800, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-second-coalition">War of the Second Coalition</a>, the French army defeated Austrian troops at the Battle of Neuburg. The town was bombed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in April 1943 and liberated by the US Army in late April 1945. On 30 June 1972 it became a Grosse Kreisstadt, similar to a county seat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A defensive wall surrounds the old town. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> ducal palace, Neuburg Castle, was built in 1530-45 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-henry-elector-palatine">Otto Henry, Elector Palatine</a>, and took its present form under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-william-elector-palatine">Philip William, Elector Palatine</a>. It houses several museums, including a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> gallery of the Bavarian State Picture Collection and an archaeology museum. Other sights are the late Renaissance court church, built in 1607/08 by Josef Heintz, the Town Hall of 1603/09, the rococo Provincial Library of 1731/32, and the baroque churches of St Peter and St Ursula. Grünau, a Renaissance hunting lodge of Otto Henry, stands 7 km further east.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neuburg an der Donau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On his death the lands were divided among his four sons, creating the electoral line and the branches of Neumarkt, Simmern-Zweibrücken and Mosbach; later extinctions repeatedly brought the electorate to collateral lines, the Simmern in 1559, the Catholic <b>Neuburg</b> in 1685 and the Sulzbach in 1742.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Known as the Young Palatinate, it had <b>Neuburg</b> on the Danube as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a></li></ul></section>
